The most practical scope problem is injecting a **request-scoped bean into a singleton**. A singleton is created once at startup — long before any HTTP request exists. If it holds a direct reference to a request-scoped bean, that reference points to nothing (or to a stale instance from a previous request).

Spring solves this with **scoped proxies**. When you declare `@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)`, Spring injects a CGLIB proxy instead of the real bean. The proxy delegates every method call to the current request's actual instance, looked up from the `RequestAttributes` at call time.

```java
@Component
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class RequestContext {
    private String correlationId;
    // each HTTP request gets its own instance
}

@Service  // singleton
public class AuditService {
    private final RequestContext ctx;  // proxy, not real bean
    public AuditService(RequestContext ctx) { this.ctx = ctx; }
    // ctx.getCorrelationId() delegates to the current request's instance
}
```

Without the `proxyMode`, Spring would try to create the `RequestContext` at startup — outside any request — and throw a `BeanCreationException` with the message 'no thread-bound request found.' This is one of the most common Spring scope errors and the scoped proxy is the standard fix.

Beyond the five built-in scopes, Spring supports **custom scopes** which are essential for tenant isolation in multi-tenant SaaS applications and batch processing contexts.

**Implementing a custom scope** requires registering a `Scope` implementation that manages object lifecycle within your custom boundary. The following demonstrates how scoping and thread-local storage interact in practice.

```java
public class TenantScope implements Scope {
    private final ThreadLocal<Map<String, Object>> scopedObjects =
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(HashMap::new);

    @Override
    public Object get(String name, ObjectFactory<?> factory) {
        Map<String, Object> scope = scopedObjects.get();
        return scope.computeIfAbsent(name, k -> factory.getObject());
    }

    @Override
    public Object remove(String name) {
        return scopedObjects.get().remove(name);
    }
    // Register: beanFactory.registerScope("tenant", new TenantScope());
}
```

**Scope proxy internals** deserve attention. When you inject a request-scoped bean into a singleton, Spring creates a CGLIB proxy that delegates to the current thread's active scope. This means every method call on the proxy involves a scope lookup — roughly 50 nanoseconds overhead per call. For hot paths, cache the result in a local variable rather than calling proxy methods repeatedly.

**Refresh scope** (from Spring Cloud) is a production pattern worth understanding. Beans annotated with `@RefreshScope` are destroyed and recreated when `/actuator/refresh` is called. This enables dynamic configuration updates without restart, but beware: all in-flight requests using the old bean instance continue with stale state until completion.

The broken version injects the prototype directly. The fixed version uses `ObjectProvider` to defer instantiation to the point of use.

**Broken — prototype injected once, shared forever:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ReportContext context;  // Injected once at startup

    public ReportService(ReportContext context) {
        this.context = context;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        context.reset();  // Attempt to reuse — NOT thread-safe
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Calling `reset()` doesn't make this safe. If two threads call `generate()` concurrently, they call `reset()` and `loadData()` on the same `context` object, interleaving their data.

**Fixed — fresh prototype on every call:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider;

    public ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider) {
        this.contextProvider = contextProvider;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        ReportContext context = contextProvider.getObject();  // New instance
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Each call to `getObject()` creates a fresh `ReportContext`. No sharing, no reset, no thread-safety concerns. The key insight is that `ObjectProvider` wraps the bean factory's prototype creation — it's functionally equivalent to `applicationContext.getBean(ReportContext.class)` but without coupling your code to the ApplicationContext API.

Remember that Spring does **not** manage prototype bean destruction. The prototype instance you get from `getObject()` is yours to manage. If it holds resources (connections, file handles), you must close them explicitly — `@PreDestroy` won't be called on prototype beans.

The first two phases happen in quick succession but are **distinct operations**. During **instantiation**, Spring calls the constructor — either the no-arg constructor or the `@Autowired` constructor. If constructor injection is used, dependencies are resolved from the context and passed as arguments, making them available from the first line of the constructor body.

The crucial difference is **field and setter injection**. These happen after the constructor returns, during the dependency injection phase. `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` uses reflection to scan the newly created object for `@Autowired` and `@Value` annotations, then injects the values. This is why reading an `@Autowired` field inside the constructor returns `null` — the field hasn't been populated yet.

```java
@Component
public class ReportService {
    @Autowired private DataSource dataSource;

    public ReportService() {
        // dataSource is NULL here — injection hasn't happened yet
        // Any code that uses dataSource will throw NullPointerException
    }

    @PostConstruct
    void init() {
        // dataSource is available here — safe to use
        dataSource.getConnection(); // works
    }
}
```

This ordering is the single most important lifecycle detail for everyday development. The fix is either to use constructor injection (dependencies are available immediately) or move initialization logic to `@PostConstruct`.

When the `ApplicationContext` shuts down — either through `context.close()` or the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring calls destruction callbacks on **singleton beans** in reverse creation order.

The callback sequence mirrors initialization: `@PreDestroy` first, then `DisposableBean.destroy()`, then `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")`. Use `@PreDestroy` for releasing resources: closing connection pools, flushing caches, deregistering JMX beans.

```java
@Component
public class ConnectionPool {
    private final HikariDataSource ds;

    @PreDestroy
    void shutdown() {
        ds.close();  // release all pooled connections
        log.info("Pool closed gracefully");
    }
}
```

The critical exception is **prototype-scoped beans**. Spring does not track prototype instances after creation — it has no reference to call `@PreDestroy` on. If a prototype bean acquires resources, the calling code must close them explicitly. This is the lifecycle gap that catches developers who switch a singleton to prototype scope without adding manual cleanup.

When a bean uses multiple initialization mechanisms, Spring fires them in a **specific order**. Understanding this order matters because it determines which callback sees which state.

**1. `@PostConstruct`** fires first. This is a JSR-250 standard annotation — it works in any Jakarta EE container, not just Spring. Dependencies are fully injected when it runs.

**2. `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`** fires second. This is Spring's older interface-based approach. It couples your code to the Spring API, so it's not recommended for new classes.

**3. `@Bean(initMethod = "setup")`** fires last. Specified as a string in the bean definition, it's called via reflection. This is the approach for third-party classes you can't annotate.

```java
@Component
public class CacheWarmer implements InitializingBean {
    @Autowired private ProductRepository repo;

    @PostConstruct
    void postConstruct() { log.info("1. @PostConstruct"); }

    @Override
    public void afterPropertiesSet() { log.info("2. afterPropertiesSet"); }

    // If registered via @Bean(initMethod="customInit")
    void customInit() { log.info("3. initMethod"); }
}
```

In practice, **use `@PostConstruct` for your own classes** and `initMethod` for third-party beans. There's rarely a reason to implement `InitializingBean` in new code.

When `ApplicationContext.close()` is called — either explicitly or through the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring iterates through all singleton beans in **reverse creation order** and calls their destruction callbacks.

The destruction sequence mirrors initialization: `@PreDestroy` first, then `DisposableBean.destroy()`, then `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")`. The reverse order ensures that beans are destroyed before the beans they depend on.

```java
@Component
public class MetricsReporter {
    private final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler;

    @PostConstruct
    void startReporting() {
        scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::report, 0, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    @PreDestroy
    void stopReporting() {
        scheduler.shutdown();
        // Wait for in-flight reports to finish
        scheduler.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

The trap is **prototype beans**. Spring creates them on demand but never tracks them — `@PreDestroy` is never called. If you switch a singleton to prototype scope without adding manual cleanup, resources leak silently. Similarly, beans registered via `@Bean` have an implicit `destroyMethod` that Spring infers by looking for `close()` or `shutdown()` methods — set `@Bean(destroyMethod = "")` to disable this if the auto-detection causes problems.

When you annotate a bean with `@Lazy`, Spring doesn't skip the bean definition — it still registers the bean in the container. What changes is the **instantiation timing**. Instead of creating the bean at startup, Spring injects a **proxy** that triggers real creation on the first method call.

```java
@Service
@Lazy
public class ReportEngine {
    public ReportEngine() {
        // This constructor runs on first use, not at startup
        loadHeavyResources();
    }
}

@Service
public class DashboardService {
    private final ReportEngine engine;

    // @Lazy on the parameter — creates a proxy for this injection point
    public DashboardService(@Lazy ReportEngine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;  // proxy, not real bean
    }

    public Report generate() {
        return engine.build();  // proxy triggers real creation here
    }
}
```

The `@Lazy` annotation can be placed on the bean class (affects all injection points) or on a specific injection point parameter (affects only that consumer). The parameter-level placement is more common because it gives fine-grained control — one service might need the bean eagerly while another can tolerate lazy initialization.

**Fix 1: Extract the shared concern.** If A needs B and B needs A, they likely share logic that belongs in a third class C. Both A and B depend on C instead of each other. This eliminates the cycle entirely and often improves cohesion.

**Fix 2: Replace one direction with events.** If A needs to notify B but B also needs to query A, replace the notification with a Spring event. A publishes, B listens. B can still depend on A for queries (one-directional), but A no longer depends on B.

```java
// Instead of: PaymentService → NotificationService → PaymentService
// Use events: PaymentService publishes, NotificationService listens
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    public void processPayment(Payment p) {
        // ... process ...
        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCompleted(p.getId()));
    }
}

@Component
public class NotificationListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onPayment(PaymentCompleted event) {
        // notification logic — no dependency on PaymentService
    }
}
```

**Fix 3: Interface segregation.** If B only needs one method from A, extract that method into an interface that A implements. B depends on the narrow interface, not on A directly. This often reveals that B didn't really need 'all of A' — it needed a specific capability.

**Why `@Lazy` is the wrong answer:** It masks the cycle by deferring creation. The code works, but the design problem remains. When someone removes the `@Lazy` annotation during refactoring (not knowing it was load-bearing), the `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException` returns.
